{
  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 26,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.598,
            0.071,
            0.813,
            0.088
          ],
          "content": "Two Variables, One Line"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.841,
            0.071,
            0.88,
            0.087
          ],
          "content": "111"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.154,
            0.111,
            0.477,
            0.13
          ],
          "content": "Consider the linear equations"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.465,
            0.132,
            0.879,
            0.152
          ],
          "content": "x - y = 1 0\\tag{1}",
          "image_source": "images/page_26_equation_3.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.416,
            0.154,
            0.878,
            0.174
          ],
          "content": "10x - 10y = 101\\tag{2}",
          "image_source": "images/page_26_equation_4.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.154,
            0.18,
            0.583,
            0.201
          ],
          "content": "Multiplying both sides of (1) by 10 gives"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.417,
            0.202,
            0.879,
            0.223
          ],
          "content": "10x - 10y = 100\\tag{3}",
          "image_source": "images/page_26_equation_6.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.229,
            0.882,
            0.288
          ],
          "content": "The left hand side in (2) and (3) are equal expressions, but the right hand side are not. Hence we cannot find values of x and y that satisfy both (2) and (3). Thus, this pair of equations has no solution!"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.136,
            0.302,
            0.399,
            0.324
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Think and Reflect"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.133,
            0.338,
            0.853,
            0.378
          ],
          "content": "Give 3 more examples of pairs of equations that have no solution. Can you find a simple rule to check when this will happen?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.154,
            0.393,
            0.432,
            0.414
          ],
          "content": "When a pair of equations"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.41,
            0.418,
            0.589,
            0.441
          ],
          "content": "a_{1}x + b_{1}y + c_{1} = 0",
          "image_source": "images/page_26_equation_11.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.41,
            0.443,
            0.588,
            0.465
          ],
          "content": "a_{2}x + b_{2}y + c_{2} = 0",
          "image_source": "images/page_26_equation_12.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.465,
            0.881,
            0.504
          ],
          "content": "(with $a _ { _ 2 } , \\; b _ { _ 2 }$ and $c _ { _ 2 }$ all not equal to 0) is such that after suitable multiplications, we can make"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.195,
            0.503,
            0.762,
            0.523
          ],
          "content": "(i) the coefficients of x of both equations equal, and"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.19,
            0.523,
            0.712,
            0.544
          ],
          "content": "(ii) the coefficients of y of both equations equal"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.153,
            0.547,
            0.879,
            0.569
          ],
          "content": "while the constants $c _ { \\mathrm { { _ 1 } } }$ and $c _ { 2 }$ remain unequal, the pair has no solution."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.154,
            0.572,
            0.798,
            0.595
          ],
          "content": "These conditions mean $a_{1}=ka_{2},b_{1}=kb_{2},c_{1}\\neq kc_{2}$ , where $k \\neq 0$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.153,
            0.595,
            0.688,
            0.636
          ],
          "content": "Hence, when $\\frac{a_{1}}{a_{2}}=\\frac{b_{1}}{b_{2}} \\neq \\frac{c_{1}}{c_{2}}$ the pair has no solution."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.643,
            0.881,
            0.722
          ],
          "content": "When $\\frac { a _ { _ 1 } } { a _ { _ 2 } } { = } \\frac { b _ { _ 1 } } { b _ { _ 2 } }$ , both the variables get eliminated during the Elimination Method. Based on what happens to the constants, the pair can either have an infinite number of solutions or no solution."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.728,
            0.882,
            0.813
          ],
          "content": "We have already seen that when one of the variables remains during the Elimination Method, there exists a unique solution. This can happen only when $\\frac { \\widetilde { a _ { _ { 1 } } }   \\neq   \\widetilde { b _ { _ { 1 } } } } { \\widetilde { a _ { _ { 2 } } } }$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.153,
            0.821,
            0.619,
            0.842
          ],
          "content": "Summarising, we have the following cases:"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.117,
            0.842,
            0.627,
            0.885
          ],
          "content": "1. when $\\frac { a _ { _ 1 } } { a _ { _ 2 } }   \\neq   \\frac { b _ { _ 1 } } { b _ { _ 2 } } ,$ the pair has a unique solution,"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
